// Pip's nineteen levels, as text. The first five are short, placed by hand; the rest — long levels, towers climbed floor
// by floor, and two in the dark — are built from pieces by `tools/pip_levels.py`. test/pip_test.wac checks that every
// lantern, flag and door in each can be reached.
//
//     .  air          #  ground        =  a plank, stood on from above and jumped through from below
//     ^  spikes       X  a block that crumbles a moment after it is stood on, and comes back
//     L  a lantern    C  a checkpoint  E  the door, open once every lantern is lit
//     P  where Pip starts              p  a beetle that patrols   h  a frog that hops at Pip
//     M  the left end of a plank that slides three tiles right and back
//     S  a spring, which throws Pip about seven tiles up
//     V  the left end of a lift: a plank that rises three tiles and comes back down
//     |  an updraft, which carries Pip up     < >  wind, which pushes Pip that way
//     b  a bat, which swoops from its perch   g  a ghost, which creeps up while Pip looks away
//     w  a crawler, which walks round whatever it is beside: floor, wall and ceiling
//
// Each is as wide as its rows and as high as it has rows, the bottom one bedrock: most are 12 high, the towers 45 to 73.

/** A level: its name, whether it is dark — lit only by its lanterns and Pip — and its rows from the top. */
export struct Level {
  string name;
  bool dark;
  string[] rows;
}

